WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.050 --> 00:00:04.837
The library is enforced in five
waves, each closing a class of risk.

2
00:00:04.837 --> 00:00:06.709
Wave one is the foundation,

3
00:00:06.709 --> 00:00:11.301
and it has no visible effect for
anyone already on modern clients.

4
00:00:11.301 --> 00:00:18.513
CA001 blocks legacy authentication, which
can't do MFA or present device state.

5
00:00:18.513 --> 00:00:23.384
Run the legacy query for at least two weeks
first and move every remaining client,

6
00:00:23.384 --> 00:00:27.674
including service accounts and
multifunction devices that send mail.

7
00:00:27.674 --> 00:00:32.045
CA020 requires a managed device
to register security information,

8
00:00:32.045 --> 00:00:36.025
so an attacker with a password
can't register their own method.

9
00:00:36.025 --> 00:00:40.430
CA021 blocks device code flow
and authentication transfer,

10
00:00:40.430 --> 00:00:43.250
the flows used in device-code phishing.

11
00:00:43.250 --> 00:00:47.087
And CA090, the probe, never moves to On.

12
00:00:47.087 --> 00:00:49.237
Wave two is administrators.

13
00:00:49.237 --> 00:00:54.116
CA003 requires the separate-device strength
with a four-hour sign-in frequency,

14
00:00:54.116 --> 00:00:58.477
targeting the Admins persona and the
highly privileged built-in roles,

15
00:00:58.477 --> 00:01:03.725
because role targeting also catches an
assignment made outside the persona process.

16
00:01:03.725 --> 00:01:10.952
CA004 requires a compliant device, CA005
blocks untrusted locations, and CA025

17
00:01:10.952 --> 00:01:17.850
blocks the admin portals and Azure
management from anything that isn't a PAW.

18
00:01:17.850 --> 00:01:21.941
That block is written as an
exclusion of devices labeled PAW:

19
00:01:21.941 --> 00:01:26.442
a positive-operator filter never
matches an unregistered device,

20
00:01:26.442 --> 00:01:30.288
so an unregistered device isn't
excluded, and it's blocked.

21
00:01:30.288 --> 00:01:36.193
CA026 gives server and Arc administration
the same assurance, and CA027 blocks

22
00:01:36.193 --> 00:01:42.099
the Azure management plane for everyone
who isn't an administrator or operator.

23
00:01:42.099 --> 00:01:48.489
Wave three is the one users notice: the
workforce MFA baseline, managed devices,

24
00:01:48.489 --> 00:01:54.878
the country block, partner and guest
policies, mobile app protection, the CA018

25
00:01:54.878 --> 00:01:56.451
catch-all, and CA038,

26
00:01:56.451 --> 00:02:01.562
without which CA011's limited
guest access isn't limited at all.

27
00:02:01.562 --> 00:02:03.924
Wave four is risk and workloads.

28
00:02:03.924 --> 00:02:07.536
Wave five is segments and the end state:

29
00:02:07.536 --> 00:02:12.000
CA022, phishing-resistant
MFA for every member.
